From 46e0801d7f52d303096dc6794af9acda07ec6666 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 05:46:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] find the file a typo meant even where a path has its letters
 by chance

With Allow typos on, a name search listed near misses only when
nothing had every word's letters in order anywhere along its path. In
a folder of any size something has them, by accident, spread over its
folders and its name. The near miss that was meant, which fff-search
had returned, was then dropped as a typo match, and the accidents
were listed. In the demo folder `raedme` listed the README.md of two
projects and neither the README.md nor the readme.txt at the top,
`badget` listed Typography.md in Brand guidelines and no Budget.csv,
and `reprot` listed Project Atlas.txt and neither report. In the
42,275 files of the cargo registry `benchmrak` listed two normalizer
benches and nothing named benchmark.

Letters in order no longer show that a word was found. A word may be
misspelled when no result has it as typed, with its letters in a row
(`may_be_misspelled`), and near misses of such a word are kept beside
the hits that have its letters in order. A result there is a hit with
every word's letters in order, so each word is judged by itself: in
`reports bduget` a path with "reports" in it and the letters of
"bduget" in order shows that "reports" is spelled right, and only
"bduget" may be a near miss. A word that a result has in a row
brings no near misses, so `main` lists no mian.txt, and a search
whose words are all such lists what it listed. A word of three
letters or fewer has no near misses, as before, and with Allow typos
off nothing changes.

`rerank` had nothing for a near miss, which ranked wherever fff-search's
score for it fell. Now a near miss of a word of six letters or more in a
hit's own name adds 30 (`near_miss_bonus`): less than a word adds that
is in the name as typed, and as a rule enough to list the name that was
meant ahead of the accidents. A shorter word gets nothing for one. One
typo in four or five letters leaves three or four of them to go by,
which many names have side by side by chance, and ranked up they bury
what the letters find in order: given the 30 at every length, `lcns`
ranked LICENSE ninth in the registry, behind libwinapi_rpcns4.a and
lens-convex.svg, where it is first, and 46 of 118 such searches ranked
their file lower. Given it from five letters, 19 of them did, six more
than do now, while 30 of the registry's 41 misspellings of five letters
had a name with their word first, where 17 have. Drawing the line at
six puts the searches with gaps before the misspellings of five
letters. A near miss that is only in a folder's name takes 30 off,
since one near miss scores much like another: in the demo folder `mian`
lists main.rs second, after the folder Many, where it was the last of
122 rows, behind the 120 files in Many. A hit that has the word's
letters in order keeps its score, unless its name has a near miss as
well.

Measured on two trees by a probe, since removed, that made each
search by the old rule and by the new in one process: the registry,
and a home-like tree of 2,996 files with long names. One typo in a
word of the tree (two letters swapped, one dropped, one doubled, one
wrong): of 237 such searches of the registry the old rule listed a
name with the word in 158 and had one first in 92, the new one in 232
and 164, and for a word of six letters or more one is first in 136 of
141, where it was in 74. In the home-like tree, of 355: listed in 311
and now in all, first in 285 and now in 338. The name was among the
hits fff-search handed over in 236 of the 237 and in all 355.

The other way round, 2,774 searches of the two trees were made as the
window makes them (those, the same words spelled right, prefixes, two
words, capitals, quotes, globs, exclusions, letters with gaps), and
10,675 more files only, by kind, in one folder and in two locations,
each with Allow typos on and off. With it off no list differs, and
with it on none does where no word is taken as misspelled, whenever
fff-search handed over the same hits both times. Where a word is,
each hit of the old list is in the new one unless the new list is
full, with the score it had, 30 more, or 30 less.

Searches for letters with gaps, which a misspelled word can't be told
from, pay for it. Of 118 that abbreviate a name of the registry (its
consonants, or its first and last two letters) 13 rank their file
lower, all of four letters: a near miss scores higher, as pkg-config
does for `pckg`, ahead of package.json. Of 131 in the home-like tree
none does. Of 120 that take three letters from a folder and four from
a name in it (`srcmain`), 13 and 7 do, twelve of the twenty by one
row.

A search with every word spelled right pays one pass over its hits
that ends at the first with the words in a row: in the registry
`cargo` takes 25 ms by the old rule and 23 by the new, and the
one-letter search 118 and 123 (debug build, least of nine runs, on a
machine the other lanes were loading). A word that may be misspelled
has `near_miss` run for each hit without its letters in order, which
the old rule did only when no hit had them: `raedme` takes 28 ms and
44, `benchmrak` 13 and 51, and `serialziation` 20 and 93, for some
2,000 hits. Built alone with `rustc -O`, `near_miss` takes about 3 ms
for 4,000 paths and a word of four letters, and about 8 ms for one of
thirteen.

Left as they are: results that keep up with the files (`changes_for`)
still pass over a new file that is only a near miss, which waits for
Refresh, as the manual says. A new file that has the words' letters in
order, and a listed one that goes, are a search's business as before,
and the search made again for one judges its words anew: a word's near
misses leave the list when a result comes that has the word as typed,
and come onto it when the last such result goes. Apps are still found
by letters in order only, so `safair` doesn't find Safari, which the
manual now says too. A near-miss row still has no highlight.

Changelog (Fixed): With **Allow typos** on, a misspelled word now
finds the name that was meant in a folder of any size. Near misses
used to be listed only when nothing at all had the word's letters in
order, and in a large folder some long path has them by accident,
spread over its folders and its name. The name that was meant was
then left out and the accidents were listed: among the sources of
some nine hundred Rust crates, `benchmrak` found two files that have
nothing to do with benchmarks and no `benchmark.rs`. Near misses of a
word are now listed whenever no result has the word as typed, with
its letters in a row, beside the results that have its letters in
order. A name with a near miss of a word of six letters or more ranks
higher, which as a rule puts the name that was meant first, and a
near miss that is only in a folder's name ranks lower, so `mian`
lists `main.rs` ahead of everything in a folder named `Many`. A word
that a result has as typed brings no near misses, a search whose
words are all such lists what it listed before, and so does every
search with **Allow typos** off. A search for letters with gaps, such
as `pckg` for `package.json`, can now list a near miss such as
`pkg-config` among its results. `fff find` follows the same rules.

