 ### Ranking and limits
 
-fff-search ranks results, and fffinder re-ranks them so name matches come first, shallow paths come before deep ones, and nothing inside `.app` or `.framework` bundles shows. Words that spell a whole name between them rank it like an exact match: `tax return` lists `Tax Return.pdf` ahead of `Tax Return 2024.pdf`. Items that match equally well are listed newest first, by when they were last modified, and then by path, so `screenshot` or `invoice` starts with the latest ones. Folders and apps, which the index keeps no date for, come after the files they tie with. A capital letter in a word is a preference: names that fit the capitals rank higher, so `README` lists `README.md` ahead of `readme.md`, and the initials `TEC` favor `TaxExemptionController.java`. When some results have every word's letters in order, the ones that match only with typos are dropped. When none has, near misses are listed instead: a word can have a typo or two, but not gaps as well (see **Allow typos** in [Settings › Search engine](#search-engine)). With **Allow typos** off, such a search finds nothing. Apps from the app folders rank as one item wherever their folder is, so "safari" or "term" puts Safari or Terminal ahead of a file with the same words in its name.
+fff-search ranks results, and fffinder re-ranks them so name matches come first, shallow paths come before deep ones, and nothing inside `.app` or `.framework` bundles shows. Words that spell a whole name between them rank it like an exact match: `tax return` lists `Tax Return.pdf` ahead of `Tax Return 2024.pdf`. Items that match equally well are listed newest first, by when they were last modified, and then by path, so `screenshot` or `invoice` starts with the latest ones. Folders and apps, which the index keeps no date for, come after the files they tie with. A capital letter in a word is a preference: names that fit the capitals rank higher, so `README` lists `README.md` ahead of `readme.md`, and the initials `TEC` favor `TaxExemptionController.java`. A search lists what has every word's letters in order and drops what matches only with typos. A word that none of those results has as typed, with its letters in a row, may be misspelled, so near misses of it are listed as well: such a word can have a typo or two, but not gaps as well (see **Allow typos** in [Settings › Search engine](#search-engine)). So `raedme` finds `README.md` even where a long path happens to have r, a, e, d, m, and e in order. A name with a near miss of a word of six letters or more ranks higher, which usually puts the name that was meant ahead of the paths that have the letters by accident. The near misses of a shorter word rank as they match, because one typo in four or five letters leaves too little to tell what was meant from what is merely close. A near miss that is only in a folder's name ranks lower, so `mian` lists `main.rs` ahead of everything in a folder named `Many`. With **Allow typos** off, only letters in order match. Apps from the app folders rank as one item wherever their folder is, so "safari" or "term" puts Safari or Terminal ahead of a file with the same words in its name. An app is found by letters in order only: a near miss doesn't list one, so `safair` doesn't find Safari.
 
 A search shows at most `result_limit` items (500 by default; **Results shown** in [Settings › Search engine](#search-engine)). A contents search counts files, each listed once however many of its lines match. Hidden files that aren't shown don't count. When more items match than are shown, the status bar says so with a `+` after the count, and a sort of the results can choose among all of them (see [Sorting and hidden files](#sorting-and-hidden-files)). With a filter on, a search fetches up to 5,000 so the filter has enough to work with. Opening a file from search results records it, so files you open often rank higher for the same search later.
 
